extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Light and dark"

append styles
	link(rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com")
	link(rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin)
	link(rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Roboto+Slab:wght@400&display=swap")

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Light and dark
				p.lead How charts look in light mode and in dark mode.

	+section("By default")
		p By default, a chart uses the text colour of the page. If the text is light in dark mode, the bars are light too. You do not need to set anything.

	+section("A colour you set")
		p A colour you set is used in light mode and in dark mode. That is fine if the colour can be read on both, such as a medium orange. If you do not use dark mode, you can stop here.

	+section("Two colours")
		p If a colour does not work on both, give it two values with #[code light-dark()]: the first for light mode, the second for dark mode. Every colour you set needs two values.

		+codeblock('CSS').
			.granny {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(#7fb03f, #a5d46a);
				--tablechart-value-color: light-dark(#5d8a2b, #c3e59a);
			}

		p The two values can be any colour: a hex value, a named CSS colour or a variable. If your site already has variables for its colours, use those.

		+codeblock('CSS').
			:root {
				--brand-green: #7fb03f;
				--brand-green-light: #a5d46a;
			}
			.granny {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(var(--brand-green), var(--brand-green-light));
			}
			.elstar {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(seagreen, palegreen);
			}

		p #[code light-dark()] only works if the page says it has a light and a dark mode, with #[code color-scheme]. To follow the setting of the system, set it on #[code :root]:

		+codeblock('CSS').
			:root {
				color-scheme: light dark;
			}

		p If your site has its own dark theme, set #[code color-scheme: dark] on the class of that theme.

	+section("Try it")
		p The buttons in the example set #[code color-scheme] on this example only. The Granny Johnson column and its number have two colours each. The other columns use one colour.

		+style.
			.scheme-example .demo {
				background: light-dark(#f7f2f5, #1d2b33);
				color: light-dark(#16323f, #e9eef1);
				--tablechart-surface: light-dark(#f7f2f5, #1d2b33);
			}
			.scheme-example .granny {
				--tablechart-bar-color: light-dark(#7fb03f, #a5d46a);
				--tablechart-value-color: light-dark(#5d8a2b, #c3e59a);
			}
			.scheme-example .tablechart-value.granny {
				font-family: "Roboto Slab", Rockwell, serif;
				font-weight: 400;
				font-size: 2.2em;
			}

		.scheme-example
			.scheme-toggle(role="group" aria-label="Colour scheme of the example")
				button(type="button" data-scheme="light" aria-pressed="true") Light
				button(type="button" data-scheme="dark" aria-pressed="false") Dark
			+chart
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption
						b Apples sold by variety
						br
						span thousands
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Elstar
								td 128
							tr.granny
								th Granny Johnson
								td 45
							tr
								th Jonagold
								td 135
							tr
								th Braeburn
								td 121

		script.
			document.querySelectorAll('.scheme-toggle button').forEach(function (button) {
				button.addEventListener('click', function () {
					var demo = document.querySelector('.scheme-example .demo');
					demo.style.colorScheme = button.dataset.scheme;
					button.parentElement.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(function (other) {
						other.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(other === button));
					});
				});
			});

	+section("The long way")
		p Without #[code light-dark()], set the variable again for dark mode. This follows the setting of the system only.

		+codeblock('CSS').
			.granny {
				--tablechart-bar-color: #7fb03f;
			}
			@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
				.granny {
					--tablechart-bar-color: #a5d46a;
				}
			}

	+section("Numbers inside bars")
		p The number inside a bar is dark or light, whichever reads on the colour of the bar. You do not need to set it for light and dark mode. To set it yourself, use #[code --tablechart-bar-ink], #[code --tablechart-up-ink], #[code --tablechart-down-ink] or #[code --tablechart-total-ink].

	+section("Background")
		p The background colour of a chart, #[code --tablechart-surface], is the background the browser uses for the page: white in light mode and dark in dark mode. Lighter lines and slices are mixed with it. If your page has another background, set #[code --tablechart-surface] with two values as well. See #[a(href=url('/styling/background.html')) Background].
